{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>When a soul sins and commits a trespass against HaShem.</b> Here sin precedes trespass, but in the case of sacrilege (verse 15) trespass precedes sin. This is because this passage deals with one who withholds his fellow's money and then swears falsely — his sin against his fellow is one sin, but against Heaven his sin is twofold, for by the act of robbery he transgressed a commandment of HaShem and then swore falsely. And this is what it says: <b>When a soul sins</b> — that is, against his fellow; <b>and commits a trespass against HaShem</b> — two acts of trespass, as stated. And the term מְעִילָה applies most fittingly toward the One above, as Rashi explained: מְעִילָה means nothing other than alteration, etc. — for HaShem is good to all, and he did not repay Him according to His deed; he alters and does not act as He acted toward him. But with respect to a fellow man, the term מְעִילָה does not apply, for what good did he do him with a deposit or with robbery? But in the case of sacrilege, trespass is placed first, for immediately, when he ate and derived benefit from consecrated property, he committed a trespass against HaShem, for he stretched forth his hand against the High Table. And the term מְעִילָה is doubled to include one who derives benefit without eating, as Rashi explained. And afterward it says, ״וְחָטְאָה״ — for he also sinned against man, for he robbed the portion of the priests, for from the consecrated things they take their share." "And by way of allusion it says, 'When a soul sins and commits a trespass against HaShem, and deals falsely with his fellow in the matter of a deposit' — for every night a man deposits his spirit into the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and even though the soul owes a debt to the Holy One, blessed be He, for through sins it is pursued to the point of obligation, nevertheless the Holy One, blessed be He, does not withhold His deposit and returns it to him in the same form as He received it. And this man who denies his fellow's deposit — even though his fellow is not indebted to him — does not repay the Holy One, blessed be He, according to His deed. And according to this, the soul that sins in the matter of his fellow's deposit commits trespass against a third party, namely against HaShem, who commanded him to return his deposit."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The phrase 'בפסוק טו' appears to be a marginal or inline reference added to the text; rendered as '(verse 15)' in parentheses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:17.664700658Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}